WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:04.680
Ever since Edwin Hubble discovered

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.720
that the universe is expanding,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.440
there has been a great debate

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.440
about the speed of this expansion,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.840
also called The Hubble constant.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.620
In 2000, scientists thought they had reached

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.500
a consensus: with a Hubble constant at about 72.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.760
But since then, two methods of calculating

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.520
the expansion rate have gotten farther

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.040
and farther apart.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.660
and some scientists think it’s too big of a difference

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.820
to just be a statistical fluke.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.900
To calculate the Hubble constant,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.440
we need both a velocity, and a distance.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.440
Velocities are easy.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.480
It requires measuring the Redshift of a galaxy

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.820
as it recedes.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.800
But distances are much harder.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.520
We need “standard candles”

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.120
-- objects that shine with an intrinsic brightness.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.300
That way, its observed brightness

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.880
could be converted to a distance.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.680
The first method used to calculate the Hubble constant,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.960
originated with Hubble.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.360
He started by measuring distances to nearby objects

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.200
and working his way out,

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:01.700
hence the name...

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.080
The first rung is within our Milky Way.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.780
Here we measure the distances to Cepheids,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.760
pulsating stars that have an intrinsic brightness.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.140
Then we look to nearby galaxies that contain both

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.960
Cepheids and another type of standard candle,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.100
Type 1A Supernovae.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.180
These exploding white dwarf stars

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.740
also have an intrinsic brightness.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.080
After calibrating these supernovae,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.760
astronomers can then move out to a third rung,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.700
to even more distant galaxies

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.860
that only contain supernovae.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.220
Linking all these measurements, a recent study

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.180
has pinned the Hubble constant at 73.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.740
Instead of starting within our galaxy and looking out,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.880
the second method looks back in time

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.380
to almost the beginning of the universe.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.000
Here we find the Cosmic Microwave Background:

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.940
the radiation leftover from the Big Bang.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.680
The CMB’s subtle variations in temperature describe

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.860
changes in the density of the Universe

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.540
right after the big bang.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.340
Instead of standard candles, physicists use

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.500
the size of the tiny fluctuations as standard yardsticks.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.640
With these yardsticks, they can calculate what

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.900
the Hubble constant would be today.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.840
Recent estimates are at about 68, significantly lower

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.700
than the cosmic distance ladder’s approach.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.480
Both camps hope to resolve this debate

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.940
in the coming years with the help of

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.260
new telescopes and instruments.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.220
But if the discrepancy in the Hubble constant remains,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.920
it could mean that our understanding of

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.480
the expansion of the universe is missing an ingredient--

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.740
that something happened between

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.280
the big bang and the present.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.100
Maybe there’s a neutrino out there

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.340
we haven’t discovered.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.400
Maybe the mysterious force “dark energy,”

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.160
which makes up a significant portion of our universe,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.280
can change in strength.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.320
Or maybe subtle flaws in one or both approaches

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.180
to the Hubble Constant are giving us the wrong answer.

